{
  "dom_id": "step-q72963",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14205615/assets/da6aa84a7b37d9b776f88a7e3b6eb4cf70c303c3c0c463abd313c51bfa0781a2.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be graphs of odd functions?",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs I, III, and IV do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, they are **not** odd functions.\n\n- On the other hand, graph II does have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graph II shows an odd function.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14205615/assets/348ca99660cff1d4db774d39b543aa7cb79cf0a1787f11046e9b8a4d72fd83e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
